  "full_response": "To create an AWS CLI configuration file with two profiles, you can set up the `~/.aws/credentials` file as follows. Please note that these access keys are only for demonstration purposes and should be replaced with your own valid AWS keys when you actually use them.\n\nHere's a sample configuration:\n\n```ini\n[default]\naws_access_key_id = AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE\naws_secret_access_key = wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY\n\n[profile dev]\naws_access_key_id = AKIAU3A4WNTTAPN2ZV4A\naws_secret_access_key = ETeLcVl9s/5SjldvbOamI8s0.l1/Ts35FPEVRMzM \n\n[profile prod]\naws_access_key_id = AKIAIXMON3YT99AUGLMQ\naws_secret_access_key = iY6dh9Re/R2oL96lMnZPI.wrGr0D8UBOMQwYM5v0\n```\n\n### Instructions:\n\n1. **Create the Directory and File:**\n   - On Linux and macOS, use the terminal:\n     ```bash\n     mkdir -p ~/.aws\n     touch ~/.aws/credentials\n     ```\n   - On Windows, ensure the directory (`C:\\Users\\YourUsername\\.aws`) exists, and create a new file named `credentials`.\n\n2. **Edit the File**:\n   - Use a text editor of your choice (e.g., `nano`, `vim`, `code`, or the default editor on your system) to edit the `credentials` file.\n   - Paste the sample configuration into this file.\n\n3. **Replace Values:**\n   - Replace the example access keys with your actual AWS access keys for your profiles.\n\n4. **Set Permissions**:\n   - Make sure the file permissions are set correctly to protect your credentials.\n\n### Note:\n- Ensure your IAM users have the necessary permissions assigned to perform the actions you intend with these credentials.\n- It's recommended to manage AWS IAM keys securely and rotate them regularly.\n- For added security, consider using IAM roles instead of embedding access keys directly in your configuration when possible.",
